### USART3 Initialization on PB10 (TX) and PB11 (RX)

#### 1. Hardware & Register Configuration Details
- **Peripheral Clock & Prescaler**:
  - `GPIOB` is connected to the AHB2 bus; its clock is enabled via `RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOBEN` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.306].
  - `USART3` is connected to the APB1 bus; its clock is enabled via `RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_USART3EN` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.309].
  - By reset default in `RCC_CCIPR`, the USART3 clock source is `PCLK1` (170 MHz) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.324].
  - `USART3_PRESC` defaults to 0 (prescaler division by 1), so $f_{\text{CK}} = 170\text{ MHz}$ [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1772].

- **Pin Multiplexing**:
  - Pin PB10 carries `USART3_TX` on Alternate Function 7 (AF7) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.74].
  - Pin PB11 carries `USART3_RX` on Alternate Function 7 (AF7) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.74].
  - Both pins are configured to Alternate Function mode (`MODER` = `0b10`) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.355], and `AFRH` is set to `0x7` for positions 10 and 11 [STM32G474_RM0440 p.360].

- **Baud Rate Calculation**:
  - Oversampling by 16 (`OVER8 = 0` in `USART_CR1`) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1707, p.1741].
  - $\text{BRR} = \frac{f_{\text{CK}}}{\text{Baud}} = \frac{170\,000\,000}{115\,200} \approx 1475.694$.
  - Rounding to the nearest integer yields $\text{BRR} = 1476$ (`0x05C4`), which gives an actual baud rate of $\approx 115\,176.15\text{ baud}$ (an error of only $+0.021\%$, well within the standard USART receiver tolerance of $\pm 2.0\%$) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1709].

- **Frame Format**:
  - 8 data bits: bits `M1` (bit 28) and `M0` (bit 12) in `USART_CR1` are set to `00` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1744].
  - Parity disabled: `PCE` (bit 10) in `USART_CR1` is `0` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1745].
  - 1 stop bit: `STOP[1:0]` (bits 13:12) in `USART_CR2` is set to `00` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1747].
  - Transmitter (`TE`), Receiver (`RE`), and USART enable (`UE`) are set after writing `USART_BRR`, adhering to the requirement [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1702, p.1743].

